# MojoLlama — Qwen3-30B Fused C Forward

## Goal
Make `qwen3_forward_c` (in cengine_batch_instr.c) produce correct output and beat llama.cpp 27.89 tok/s.

## Current State
- MojoLlama achieves 25.1 agg tok/s (90%) on Qwen3-30B vs llama.cpp 27.89
- The fused C forward was created but produces all-NaN output
- The function uses #pragma omp parallel across all 48 layers
- QKV projections use quant_matmul_omp (from quant_kernels_omp.c)
- GQA uses inline non-BLAS AVX2 attention (was BLAS SGEMM but caused thread conflicts)
- MoE dispatch uses moe_forward_omp (from quant_kernels_omp.c)
- Output projection uses quant_matmul_omp

## Known Issues
1. `qwen3_forward_c` produces NaN on ALL outputs, even with OMP_NUM_THREADS=1
2. Individual quant_matmul_omp calls work correctly from Python (max=183.19, no NaN)
3. The bug is in the C function's internal logic, not the Python wiring
4. Non-BLAS GQA was added to avoid OpenBLAS thread conflicts within #pragma omp parallel
5. The RMS norm uses #pragma omp atomic for rms_ss accumulation

## Model Architecture (Qwen3-30B)
- 48 layers, N=2048, NH=32, n_kv_h=4, HD=128, FF=6144
- 128 experts, top_k=8, n_ff_expert=768
- Attention weights: Q4_K (type 12)
- Expert gate/up: Q4_K (type 12), Expert down: Q6_K (type 14)
- Output weight: Q8_0 (type 8), shape [151936, 2048]

## Files
- cengine_batch_instr.c: Contains qwen3_forward_c at end of file
- quant_kernels_omp.c: quant_matmul_omp, moe_forward_omp, and all quantized matmul kernels
- turbo_engine_v7_moe.py: Python engine

